@page "/"

<PageTitle>Blazix.Primitives sample application</PageTitle>

<main class="Home">
    <h1>Blazix.Primitives sample application</h1>
    <p>
        One page per family, each porting the hero demo of the Base UI documentation, for trying the port by hand. Every
        page exists twice: under <code>/server/&lt;family&gt;</code> it runs on Blazor Server, under
        <code>/wasm/&lt;family&gt;</code> on Blazor WebAssembly. Both prerender first, as an app a user visits does.
    </p>

    <table class="FamilyTable">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Family</th>
                <th scope="col">Blazor Server</th>
                <th scope="col">Blazor WebAssembly</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (DemoEntry entry in DemoCatalog.Entries)
            {
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">
                        @entry.Title
                        <small>@entry.Summary</small>
                    </th>
                    <td><a href="/server/@entry.Slug">/server/@entry.Slug</a></td>
                    <td><a href="/wasm/@entry.Slug">/wasm/@entry.Slug</a></td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Which mode a page runs in</h2>
    <p>
        The first segment of the address decides it. The first Server visit opens a circuit; the first WebAssembly visit
        downloads the runtime, which is slow once and cached after. This page is static. On a demo page the badge under the
        title shows the mode that was asked for and flips from <em>prerendered</em> to <em>interactive</em> once Blazor has
        taken the page over, so a page that stays <em>prerendered</em> is a page whose mode did not start.
    </p>
</main>
